{
    "name": "WES tertiary analysis from VCF (v2)",
    "a_galaxy_workflow": "true",
    "format-version": "0.1",
    "annotation": "Tertiary analysis of a germline whole exome sequencing (WES) VCF.\n\n1. Normalise the calls against the reference: split multiallelic records, decompose complex alleles (MNVs)\n   into SNVs/indels, left-align indels and drop records whose REF does not match the reference (bcftools norm).\n2. Keep calls inside the exome target regions, padded by a configurable number of bases so that\n   canonical splice sites are retained, and that pass the quality expression (bcftools filter).\n3. Add ClinVar clinical significance (CLNSIG, CLNREVSTAT, CLNDN, CLNSIGCONF, ALLELEID) and the\n   gnomAD v4.1 population frequencies (INFO/gnomAD_AF, grpmax AF, filtering AF FAF95, homozygote count, gnomAD\n   filter and exome/genome discordance) and the AlphaMissense missense score (INFO/AM_score) with bcftools\n   annotate; score the rare variants with SpliceAI (INFO/SpliceAI).\n4. Predict functional consequences (gene, effect, impact, HGVS c./p., LOF/NMD) with SnpEff.\n5. Prioritise candidates with a SnpSift expression. The default keeps ClinVar (likely) pathogenic\n   variants at any frequency, plus rare (gnomAD AF and grpmax FAF95 < 1% or absent) HIGH/MODERATE impact variants.\n6. Export tab-separated tables of all annotated variants and of the candidates, and variant statistics\n   (bcftools stats, SnpEff summary) for MultiQC.\n7. Runs of homozygosity (bcftools roh, using the population allele frequencies).\n8. Clinical interpretation report (HTML) and findings table: diagnostic ClinVar pathogenic/likely pathogenic\n   findings checked against the gene's mode of inheritance (GenCC), candidate loss-of-function variants with\n   automated ACMG/AMP evidence codes and optional HPO phenotype ranking, ACMG SF v3.3 secondary findings,\n   copy-number variants with ClinGen dosage and CNV points (when the CNV calls are given),\n   carrier status, pharmacogenomics (CPIC genes, and PharmCAT 3.4.0 diplotypes with its own HTML report), risk alleles, runs of homozygosity, coverage of clinically\n   relevant genes and the limits of the analysis. The optional inputs (GenCC, gnomAD constraint, HPO, BAM,\n   coverage) refine the report; the FASTQ workflow connects all of them.\n\nMap this workflow over a collection of VCFs to process a batch of samples.\nReference FASTA, target BED, ClinVar and gnomAD must use the same genome build and the same\nchromosome naming (e.g. \"chr1\" vs \"1\") as the VCF.\n",
    "tags": [
        "WES",
        "exome",
        "variant-annotation",
        "variant-prioritization",
        "tertiary-analysis"
    ],
    "license": "MIT",
    "release": "0.8",
    "uuid": "325a3624-2876-419f-9805-0558388e1825",
    "comments": [],
